const groups = [
 {title:'The foundations',desc:'Java, the web, and the ideas everything else builds on.',lessons:5},
 {title:'Getting started with Spring Boot',desc:'Set up your tools and understand what Spring does for you.',lessons:5},
 {title:'Build a REST API',desc:'Turn requests into useful responses, one endpoint at a time.',lessons:5},
 {title:'Work with data',desc:'Store, query, and safely update real application data.',lessons:5},
 {title:'Testing & security',desc:'Validate input, handle errors, test, and secure your API.',lessons:5},
 {title:'From project to production',desc:'Package, deploy, troubleshoot, and put it all together.',lessons:5}
];
const lessons=[];
const code=(label,text)=>({label,text});
function add(id,title,minutes,intro,sections,example,explain,exercise,solution,reference){lessons.push({id,title,minutes,intro,sections,example,explain,exercise,solution,reference});}
add('what-is-spring','What is Spring Boot?',12,
"If you’ve never built a backend, start here. We’ll look at what happens when an app sends a request and where Spring Boot fits in.",
[['Start with a familiar action','Imagine a task app. You type “Review Java” and press Add. The interface sends a request to a backend program. That program checks the input, stores the task, and replies. Spring Boot helps you build that backend in Java. A backend can serve a website, a mobile app, or another program; it does not have to draw a web page.'],['Java, Spring, and Boot','<strong>Java</strong> is the programming language. <strong>Spring Framework</strong> provides tools for organizing Java objects and handling web requests. <strong>Spring Boot</strong> builds on Spring with dependency starters, automatic configuration, and a server you can run inside your application. Boot reduces setup; your code still defines what the application does.'],['The project you will build','Throughout this guide, build a Task API: create tasks, list them, update completion, and delete them. Begin with a simple greeting, then add a database, validation, tests, and production configuration. Read in order, type the examples, and do the exercises. The final lesson includes a complete downloadable starting point.']],
code('A request and its response','GET /api/tasks/1\n\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{"id":1,"title":"Review Java","completed":false}'),
'The request asks for task 1. The status 200 means the request succeeded. The response body is JSON: a text format for named values. This is a preview, not Java code to run.',
'In your own words, describe what happens between pressing Add in a task app and seeing the new task. Identify what the frontend and backend each do.',
'The frontend collects the title and sends a request. The backend validates it, saves a task, and returns the saved result. The frontend displays that result. Spring Boot helps implement the backend portion.',
'https://spring.io/projects/spring-boot');
add('java-basics','Java basics',25,
"Let’s cover the Java you’ll need for the examples: variables, types, methods, and loops. If you already know Java, you can use this lesson as a quick refresher.",
[['Values, variables, and types','<code>int</code> stores whole numbers, <code>boolean</code> stores true or false, and <code>String</code> stores text. Java checks types before running your program. Use <code>String</code> with a capital S, double quotes for strings, and a semicolon after a statement.'],['Decisions and repetition','An <code>if</code> statement selects a branch. A <code>for</code> loop repeats work. Compare string contents using <code>.equals()</code>; <code>==</code> compares object references. Methods declare their return type; <code>void</code> means no value is returned.'],['Run a tiny program','Install a Java 21 JDK in the setup lesson if needed. Save this example as <code>Main.java</code>. In its folder, run <code>javac Main.java</code>, then <code>java Main</code>. The first command compiles source into bytecode; the second runs it on the Java Virtual Machine (JVM).']],
code('Main.java','public class Main {\n    static String label(String title, boolean completed) {\n        if (completed) {\n            return title + " ✓";\n        }\n        return title + " (todo)";\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {\n            System.out.println(label("Task " + i, false));\n        }\n    }\n}'),
'The JVM starts at main. The loop calls label three times. Each call receives its own parameters and returns text. The output is Task 1 (todo), Task 2 (todo), and Task 3 (todo), on separate lines.',
'Change the loop so only Task 2 is completed. Predict the output before running it.',
'Pass i == 2 as the second argument: label("Task " + i, i == 2). This boolean expression is true only for the second iteration. Task 2 gets a check mark; the other two remain todo.',
'https://dev.java/learn/language-basics/');
add('objects','Classes, objects & interfaces',25,
"A class describes something your program works with. An object is one instance of that class. We’ll use tasks as an example, then look at how interfaces help objects work together.",
[['State and behavior','Fields hold object state; methods expose behavior. A constructor initializes a new instance. <code>private</code> hides fields from callers, while public methods provide a controlled way to interact with them. <code>this</code> refers to the current instance. Each task object can have a different title.'],['Interfaces are contracts','An interface describes methods without requiring a particular implementation. For example, a NotificationSender can have a send method while EmailSender and FakeSender implement it differently. This makes substitution and testing easier. Use interfaces where the abstraction is useful; not every class needs one.'],['Records and immutability','A Java record is a compact data carrier. <code>record TaskResponse(long id, String title) {}</code> creates a constructor and accessors named <code>id()</code> and <code>title()</code>. Records work well for API messages. They are not a drop-in replacement for mutable JPA entities.']],
code('Task.java','public class Task {\n    private final String title;\n    private boolean completed;\n\n    public Task(String title) {\n        this.title = title;\n    }\n\n    public void complete() {\n        completed = true;\n    }\n\n    public boolean isCompleted() {\n        return completed;\n    }\n}'),
'new Task("Study") creates an instance. Its completed field starts as false. Calling complete() changes that instance. final prevents reassignment of title after construction; it does not make the entire object immutable.',
'Create two tasks in a main method. Complete the first, then print both completion values.',
'Task first = new Task("Java"); Task second = new Task("Spring"); first.complete(); System.out.println(first.isCompleted()); System.out.println(second.isCompleted()); prints true and false. The objects have independent state.',
'https://dev.java/learn/classes-objects/');
add('collections','Collections, null & exceptions',25,
"What if you have a list of tasks, or someone asks for a task that doesn’t exist? Collections, Optional, and exceptions help you handle those situations.",
[['Choose a collection','A <code>List&lt;Task&gt;</code> is an ordered sequence that can contain duplicates. A <code>Set</code> represents unique elements. A <code>Map&lt;Long, Task&gt;</code> associates IDs with tasks. The angle brackets specify element types, so the compiler catches incompatible values. <code>List.of()</code> produces an unmodifiable list.'],['Missing is a real possibility','<code>null</code> means there is no referenced object. Calling a method on null throws NullPointerException. An <code>Optional&lt;Task&gt;</code> explicitly models a present or missing result. Prefer <code>orElseThrow()</code> or a deliberate default over blindly calling <code>get()</code>.'],['Failure and exceptions','An exception interrupts normal control flow. Catch it when you can recover or translate it meaningfully. Do not catch every exception and pretend success. A RuntimeException is unchecked; checked exceptions must be caught or declared. Later, translate “task missing” into an HTTP 404.']],
code('Collection example (inside a method)','Map<Long, String> tasks = new HashMap<>();\ntasks.put(1L, "Learn Java");\ntasks.put(2L, "Learn Spring");\n\nOptional<String> found = Optional.ofNullable(tasks.get(3L));\nSystem.out.println(found.orElse("No task found"));\n\nfor (String title : tasks.values()) {\n    System.out.println(title);\n}'),
'Import Map, HashMap, and Optional from java.util. 1L is a long literal. The missing ID produces “No task found”. HashMap does not promise the order in which its values are visited.',
'Look up ID 2. Then change the example to throw IllegalArgumentException when the ID is missing.',
'ID 2 contains “Learn Spring”. For a deliberate failure, use Optional.ofNullable(tasks.get(3L)).orElseThrow(() -> new IllegalArgumentException("Task not found"));. The lambda supplies the exception when needed.',
'https://dev.java/learn/collections-framework/');
add('http','How the web actually works',20,
"Before writing an endpoint, it helps to know what a request contains. Let’s break down URLs, HTTP methods, status codes, and JSON.",
[['Read a URL','In <code>http://localhost:8080/api/tasks?completed=false</code>, localhost means your own computer, 8080 is the port, /api/tasks is the path, and completed=false is a query parameter. The server listens for requests on that port. A port is not a folder.'],['Methods and status codes','GET reads; POST commonly creates; PUT replaces a resource representation; PATCH partially changes it; DELETE removes it. Use 200 for a successful response with a body, 201 for creation, 204 for success without a body, 400 for malformed input, 401 for missing or invalid authentication, 403 for forbidden access, 404 for missing resources, and 500 for unexpected server failure.'],['Headers and JSON','Headers carry metadata. Content-Type describes the body format; Accept expresses a preferred response format. JSON uses double-quoted property names, supports arrays and objects, and has no comments. It is text on the wire, not a Java object. Serialization converts an object to JSON; deserialization converts JSON into an object.']],
code('HTTP request — conceptual, not a shell command','POST /api/tasks HTTP/1.1\nHost: localhost:8080\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{"title":"Learn HTTP"}'),
'The request line specifies a method and path. Headers follow, then a blank line, then the body. Typing a URL into a browser normally sends GET; use curl or an API client for POST, PUT, and DELETE.',
'Pick the appropriate status for: created a task, requested an unknown ID, sent an empty required title, deleted a task with no response body.',
'Use 201 Created, 404 Not Found, 400 Bad Request, and 204 No Content respectively. A successful HTTP exchange can still contain an application error, so check the status rather than assuming every response is success.',
'https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Overview');
add('setup','Set up your workspace',25,
"Let’s get the project running on your computer. We’ll use Java 21, Maven, and Spring Boot 4.1.x, with instructions for Windows and macOS or Linux.",
[['Install and verify Java','Install a Java 21 JDK, for example from Eclipse Temurin. A JDK includes development tools; a runtime alone is not enough. Run <code>java -version</code> and <code>javac -version</code>. Both should report 21. Select the same JDK in your editor. If they disagree, check your PATH and JAVA_HOME. IntelliJ IDEA, VS Code with Java extensions, or Spring Tools can open the project.'],['Generate a project','Open <a href="https://start.spring.io" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Spring Initializr</a>. Choose Maven, Java, a stable 4.1.x release, Jar packaging, and Java 21. Use group <code>com.example</code>, artifact <code>task-api</code>, and package <code>com.example.taskapi</code>. Add Spring Web, Spring Data JPA, Validation, and H2 Database. Generate, unzip, and open the folder containing pom.xml. Avoid milestone and snapshot releases for this course.'],['Start it locally','The generated Maven wrapper downloads and runs a suitable Maven version. Keep its files. On Windows PowerShell use <code>.\\mvnw.cmd spring-boot:run</code>; on macOS/Linux use <code>./mvnw spring-boot:run</code>. The first run needs internet access. Wait for the Started message. Stop with Ctrl+C. A 404 at the root path is normal before a controller exists.']],
code('Verify your tools','java -version\njavac -version\n\n# macOS / Linux, in your project folder\n./mvnw spring-boot:run\n\n# Windows PowerShell (alternative)\n.\\mvnw.cmd spring-boot:run'),
'Run the command for your operating system, not both. Boot starts an embedded web server on port 8080 by default. If the port is occupied, stop the other application or set server.port=8081 in application.properties.',
'Generate and start your project. Find the port and the Started message in the logs. Open http://localhost:8080 and explain the response.',
'An application with no endpoint for / returns 404. This means the server is responding, but no handler matches the requested path. It is not a failed Java installation.',
'https://docs.spring.io/spring-boot/system-requirements.html');
add('project-structure','Find your way around a project',18,
"You’ve downloaded a project. Now what? Here’s where your Java files, settings, and tests go, and what the generated files are for.",
[['Source, resources, and tests','Java application code belongs under <code>src/main/java</code>. Configuration belongs in <code>src/main/resources</code>. Test code belongs in <code>src/test/java</code>. Maven writes generated output to <code>target</code>; do not edit those files. Package names match directories under the Java source root.'],['Your application entry point','The generated TaskApiApplication class contains main and @SpringBootApplication. Put it in the root package com.example.taskapi. Place controllers, services, and repositories in that package or its subpackages so Spring can discover them. Code outside the scan boundary may compile but never become a Spring bean.'],['Dependencies and the build','The pom.xml describes your project and dependencies. A starter is a curated dependency bundle. Let the Spring Boot parent manage compatible versions instead of manually versioning every Spring library. In Boot 4, the MVC web starter is <code>spring-boot-starter-webmvc</code>; generated projects may differ from older tutorials.']],
code('Project layout','task-api/\n  pom.xml\n  mvnw\n  mvnw.cmd\n  src/main/java/com/example/taskapi/\n    TaskApiApplication.java\n    HelloController.java\n  src/main/resources/\n    application.properties\n  src/test/java/com/example/taskapi/\n    TaskApiApplicationTests.java'),
'Create HelloController.java alongside the application class in the next lesson. Its first line must be package com.example.taskapi;. Your IDE may create the correct folder when you create the package.',
'Where should application.properties go? What happens if you put a controller in com.other instead of com.example.taskapi?',
'Configuration belongs in src/main/resources. com.other is outside the default component scan rooted at com.example.taskapi, so the controller is not discovered unless you explicitly configure scanning. Prefer keeping application code under the root package.',
'https://docs.spring.io/spring-boot/reference/using/structuring-your-code.html');
add('hello','Your first working endpoint',20,
"Let’s write an endpoint you can actually call. You’ll add a controller, start the app, and open a URL to see its response.",
[['Create the controller','Add the complete file below next to TaskApiApplication.java. An annotation is metadata attached to code. @RestController tells Spring that this class handles web requests and its return values belong in the response body. @GetMapping connects a GET request path to a method.'],['Follow the request','Visit <code>http://localhost:8080/hello?name=Matthew</code>. Spring matches /hello, reads the name parameter, invokes hello, and converts the returned record to JSON using its configured message converter. You did not write a socket loop or manually build JSON text.'],['Change one thing','Try the same URL without a query parameter. The default value becomes World. If you get 404, check the path and package. If changes do not appear, restart the app; automatic reload is not assumed. The example returns JSON, so it is not a page template.']],
code('HelloController.java','package com.example.taskapi;\n\nimport org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;\nimport org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestParam;\nimport org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;\n\n@RestController\npublic class HelloController {\n    public record Greeting(String message) {}\n\n    @GetMapping("/hello")\n    public Greeting hello(\n            @RequestParam(defaultValue = "World") String name) {\n        return new Greeting("Hello, " + name + "!");\n    }\n}'),
'The response for name=Matthew is {"message":"Hello, Matthew!"}. The method name hello does not define the URL; the mapping annotation does. This controller does not require a database.',
'Add a GET /ping endpoint returning {"message":"pong"}. Keep /hello working.',
'Add a second method inside the controller: @GetMapping("/ping") public Greeting ping() { return new Greeting("pong"); }. Restart and request /ping. Both methods can share the same response record.',
'https://spring.io/guides/gs/rest-service/');
add('injection','Beans & dependency injection',25,
"A controller often needs another object to do some work. Instead of creating that object itself, it can ask Spring to provide one. That’s dependency injection.",
[['What is a bean?','A bean is an object managed by the Spring application context. @Component marks a general component. @Service expresses business logic. @Repository expresses data access. @RestController is also discovered as a component. Spring creates them and resolves their dependencies. An object you create yourself with new is not automatically a managed bean.'],['Constructor injection','If TaskController needs TaskService, declare it as a constructor parameter and store it in a final field. Spring supplies the service. With a single constructor, @Autowired is unnecessary. This makes dependencies visible and lets tests create the object directly. Avoid using field injection just to save a few lines.'],['Scope and thread safety','Most beans are singletons: one managed instance per application context, not one per request. Several requests can use the same service concurrently. Do not store per-user request state in mutable service fields. Circular constructor dependencies often mean responsibilities need to be split.']],
code('Two files in com.example.taskapi','// GreetingService.java\npackage com.example.taskapi;\nimport org.springframework.stereotype.Service;\n\n@Service\npublic class GreetingService {\n    public String greet(String name) {\n        return "Hello, " + name;\n    }\n}\n\n// In a controller: constructor injection pattern\nprivate final GreetingService greetings;\n\npublic HelloController(GreetingService greetings) {\n    this.greetings = greetings;\n}'),
'The second block is a fragment to place inside HelloController, not a separate complete file. The controller can call greetings.greet(name). Outside Spring, a test can instantiate new HelloController(new GreetingService()).',
'Why does constructing new GreetingService() inside every controller make replacing it in a test harder?',
'The controller chooses the concrete collaborator itself, so a test cannot supply a fake through its public construction contract. Constructor injection lets the caller provide the collaborator and keeps the controller focused on handling requests.',
'https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/dependencies/factory-collaborators.html');
add('configuration','Auto-configuration & settings',22,
"Boot sets up a lot for you. Here’s how it decides what to configure, where to put your settings, and how to change a default when you need to.",
[['What Boot configures','@SpringBootApplication combines configuration, component scanning, and auto-configuration. If the MVC web libraries are available, Boot configures web infrastructure. With JPA and a suitable database driver, it can configure database access. These are conditional defaults; user-defined beans can cause particular defaults to back off.'],['Keep settings outside your Java code','Put local non-secret defaults in application.properties. Environment variables and command-line arguments can override many settings. Use profiles for environment-specific behavior: application-dev.properties and application-prod.properties. Do not commit credentials. A profile is configuration selection, not a security boundary.'],['Investigate rather than guess','Start with the debug flag to inspect the condition evaluation report when you need to understand auto-configuration. Read the first meaningful cause in a startup stack trace. For several related custom settings, prefer validated @ConfigurationProperties over scattered @Value fields.']],
code('src/main/resources/application.properties','spring.application.name=task-api\nserver.port=8080\n\n# Local learning database: contents vanish when the app stops\nspring.datasource.url=jdbc:h2:mem:tasks\nspring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=create-drop\nspring.jpa.open-in-view=false\n\n# Useful while learning; avoid excessive production logging\nlogging.level.com.example.taskapi=DEBUG'),
'create-drop is intentionally destructive and only for this temporary learning database. open-in-view=false keeps database access out of response rendering. Production schema changes use migrations, introduced later.',
'Run on port 8081 without editing the configuration file.',
'On macOS/Linux: ./mvnw spring-boot:run -Dspring-boot.run.arguments=--server.port=8081. On Windows: .\\mvnw.cmd spring-boot:run "-Dspring-boot.run.arguments=--server.port=8081". Then visit port 8081, not 8080.',
'https://docs.spring.io/spring-boot/reference/features/external-config.html');
add('rest-design','Design your Task API',20,
"Before we write the Task API, let’s decide how it should behave. We need URLs for creating, finding, updating, and deleting tasks, plus a clear response for each one.",
[['Use resources and HTTP methods','A task is a resource. Use /api/tasks for its collection and /api/tasks/{id} for one task. Let the HTTP method communicate the operation instead of paths such as /createTask. REST is an architectural style, not a synonym for any JSON endpoint. Consistent contracts matter more than clever naming.'],['Decide the operations','Our API creates tasks with a title and completed=false, returns task representations, fully replaces editable fields using PUT, and deletes tasks. A successful creation returns 201 with a Location header pointing to the new resource. PUT requires both title and completed; PATCH would need explicit missing-field semantics.'],['What happens if a request is sent twice?','GET is safe: it should not change application state. PUT and DELETE are idempotent in intended effect: repeating them does not keep adding changes. Responses may differ across repeated DELETE calls. POST creation is not inherently idempotent; a retry can create another task.']],
code('Our API contract','GET    /api/tasks       -> 200  task array\nGET    /api/tasks/1     -> 200  task, or 404\nPOST   /api/tasks       -> 201  created task + Location\nPUT    /api/tasks/1     -> 200  updated task, or 404\nDELETE /api/tasks/1     -> 204  no body, or 404\n\nPOST body: {"title":"Learn REST"}\nPUT body:  {"title":"Learn REST","completed":true}'),
'This guide intentionally starts with an unpaginated collection for a small learning project. The pagination lesson shows what to change before serving a large collection.',
'Why should GET /api/tasks/1/complete not be used to mark a task completed?',
'GET is expected to be safe. Browsers, caches, or link prefetchers can issue it without intending a write. Use a state-changing method such as PUT or a carefully designed PATCH instead.',
'https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Methods');
add('request-input','Paths, queries & request bodies',22,
"A value can arrive in a URL, a query parameter, or a JSON body. Spring uses different annotations to read each of these. Let’s see when to use which one.",
[['Three places for input','@PathVariable reads a segment such as 42 from /api/tasks/42. @RequestParam reads a query value such as completed=true. @RequestBody deserializes a body, usually JSON. Headers are separate and can be accessed with @RequestHeader.'],['Conversions can fail','Binding "abc" to a Long ID fails before your service is called and normally produces 400. Missing required parameters also produce an error. A syntactically valid number might still refer to no task; that is a separate 404 condition your service must handle.'],['Be explicit about names','Use @PathVariable("id") and @RequestParam(name="completed", required=false) for clear bindings. A Boolean wrapper can represent an omitted filter as null; a primitive boolean cannot. Do not assume an absent filter means false unless your contract says so.']],
code('Controller method fragments','@GetMapping("/{id}")\npublic TaskResponse get(@PathVariable("id") Long id) {\n    return service.get(id);\n}\n\n@PostMapping\npublic ResponseEntity<TaskResponse> create(\n        @Valid @RequestBody CreateTaskRequest request) {\n    TaskResponse saved = service.create(request);\n    return ResponseEntity\n        .created(URI.create("/api/tasks/" + saved.id()))\n        .body(saved);\n}'),
'These methods go inside the TaskController built later. TaskResponse and CreateTaskRequest are our own types, not Spring classes. @Valid triggers validation once the validation dependency and constraints are present.',
'For /api/tasks/42?verbose=true with a JSON body, identify which annotation reads 42, which reads verbose, and which reads the body.',
'@PathVariable("id") reads 42 from a /{id} mapping; @RequestParam("verbose") reads the query value; @RequestBody reads JSON. For GET, keep the API body-free rather than relying on a request body that clients may not support.',
'https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/web/webmvc/mvc-controller/ann-methods.html');
add('dto','DTOs & JSON responses',20,
"The data you accept from a client doesn’t have to look exactly like your database row. DTOs let you choose what clients can send and what they get back.",
[['Separate incoming and outgoing data','CreateTaskRequest contains what callers may supply. TaskResponse includes the generated ID and stored completion state. Do not accept a database entity directly just because it has the same fields today. Otherwise future internal fields can accidentally become writable or visible.'],['Use records for simple messages','Records describe immutable data carriers with named components. JSON property names match those components under the default conventions. A nested object can become another DTO; a JSON array can become a List. Keep sensitive fields out of response DTOs instead of hoping clients ignore them.'],['Return the right status','Returning a plain object normally produces 200. ResponseEntity lets you choose headers and status as well as the body. For deletion, return ResponseEntity.noContent().build(). Do not return a body with 204.']],
code('Each public record belongs in its own named .java file','package com.example.taskapi;\n\npublic record CreateTaskRequest(String title) {}\n\n// TaskResponse.java — repeat the package declaration\npublic record TaskResponse(Long id, String title, boolean completed) {}\n\n// UpdateTaskRequest.java — repeat the package declaration\npublic record UpdateTaskRequest(String title, Boolean completed) {}'),
'This listing displays three files together for readability; split them into CreateTaskRequest.java, TaskResponse.java, and UpdateTaskRequest.java. Boolean allows validation to distinguish omitted completed from false. Constraints are added in lesson 21.',
'Why should a client not supply the database-generated task ID when creating a task?',
'The server owns ID assignment. Accepting arbitrary IDs can create collisions or unintended updates, and it blurs the difference between creation and replacement. Exclude ID from the create DTO and return it in TaskResponse.',
'https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/web/webmvc/mvc-controller/ann-methods/responseentity.html');
add('layers','Controller → service → repository',25,
"As the API grows, putting everything in a controller gets hard to follow. Let’s split the work between a controller, a service, and a repository.",
[['The controller speaks HTTP','The controller selects methods and paths, accepts DTOs, triggers input validation, and chooses response statuses. It should not contain SQL, database transaction details, or all of your business rules.'],['The service expresses behavior','The service implements use cases such as “create a task” and “complete a task”. It loads state, checks business rules, changes entities, and converts results into DTOs. Constructor injection supplies the repository. Transaction boundaries usually belong here.'],['The repository handles persistence','A repository retrieves and saves entities. Spring Data can generate an implementation from an interface. Do not return entities from controllers just to skip mapping: that can expose internal fields and trigger unintended database access during serialization. For a tiny example this feels like extra structure; it pays off as rules grow.']],
code('TaskService.java — structural preview','@Service\npublic class TaskService {\n    private final TaskRepository repository;\n\n    public TaskService(TaskRepository repository) {\n        this.repository = repository;\n    }\n\n    public TaskResponse create(CreateTaskRequest request) {\n        Task saved = repository.save(new Task(request.title()));\n        return new TaskResponse(\n            saved.getId(), saved.getTitle(), saved.isCompleted());\n    }\n}'),
'This is a preview of types built in the next lessons. Import Service from org.springframework.stereotype. The complete capstone includes package declarations, imports, and every method together.',
'Where should “a task cannot be completed until it has a title” live? Where should “return HTTP 201” live?',
'The business invariant belongs in the service or domain model, so it also applies outside HTTP. Request validation can reject a blank title early. HTTP 201 belongs in the controller, because it is a transport concern.',
'https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/core/beans/classpath-scanning.html');
add('api-client','Call and debug your API',20,
"Let’s send some requests and check the results. A browser is fine for a simple GET, but curl or an API client lets you send JSON and use other HTTP methods.",
[['Choose a client','A browser is enough for GET /hello. For bodies and methods, use curl, Postman, or an editor HTTP client. On Windows use curl.exe to avoid historical PowerShell aliases; JSON quoting varies between shells, so Invoke-RestMethod is often simpler.'],['Read the evidence','curl -i includes response headers. Confirm the status, Content-Type, and Location. A connection-refused error means you did not reach a listening server; a 404 means a server answered but the resource or mapping was missing. A 405 means the path exists for a different method.'],['Build a small request sequence','After implementing the Task endpoints, create a task, copy its returned ID, read it, update it, and delete it. Do not assume the first ID is always 1. Keep these requests alongside your project so regressions are easy to reproduce.']],
code('Run after the Task API is implemented','# macOS / Linux\ncurl -i -X POST http://localhost:8080/api/tasks \\\n  -H \'Content-Type: application/json\' \\\n  -d \'{"title":"Practice Spring"}\'\n\n# Windows PowerShell\nInvoke-RestMethod -Method Post `\n  -Uri http://localhost:8080/api/tasks `\n  -ContentType "application/json" `\n  -Body \'{"title":"Practice Spring"}\''),
'These are alternative commands for different shells. The JSON title becomes a CreateTaskRequest. Expect 201 from curl and a task representation. Invoke-RestMethod displays the deserialized response body.',
'Send the creation request without Content-Type, then with broken JSON, then with a valid body. Compare what happens.',
'With curl -d, omitting the JSON header normally sends form content and produces 415 Unsupported Media Type for this endpoint. Malformed JSON with the correct header produces 400. A valid body produces 201 after the controller and service exist.',
'https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html');
add('databases','Databases, SQL & JPA',25,
"Our tasks need somewhere to live. We’ll start with a small database and look at how JPA connects Java objects to its tables.",
[['Tables, rows, and keys','A tasks table contains one row per task. Columns hold id, title, and completed. A primary key uniquely identifies a row. SQL SELECT reads rows, INSERT creates them, UPDATE changes them, and DELETE removes them. Constraints such as NOT NULL protect integrity even when another program writes to the database.'],['The persistence stack','JPA is a Java persistence specification. Hibernate is the provider Boot commonly uses to implement it. Spring Data JPA adds repository abstractions on top. JDBC is a lower-level Java database access API. These names describe different layers, not interchangeable products.'],['Start small with H2','H2 is convenient for local learning. Our in-memory database is recreated whenever the application starts and disappears when it stops. A real deployed application needs a persistent database and a deliberate schema migration strategy. Do not mistake successful H2 tests for proof that PostgreSQL behaves identically.']],
code('SQL mental model','CREATE TABLE tasks (\n  id BIGINT GENERATED BY DEFAULT AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n  title VARCHAR(120) NOT NULL,\n  completed BOOLEAN NOT NULL\n);\n\nINSERT INTO tasks (title, completed) VALUES (\'Learn JPA\', false);\nSELECT id, title, completed FROM tasks WHERE completed = false;'),
'This SQL explains the table shape. In the local demo Hibernate generates the schema, so do not also execute this CREATE TABLE against the same table. Later Flyway takes ownership of schema creation.',
'If you stop and restart the app, why are tasks gone? Would changing only the Java collection type fix this?',
'The jdbc:h2:mem URL creates an in-memory database, and create-drop recreates the schema. Java collection choice does not change durability. Use persistent storage and migrations when you need data to survive restarts.',
'https://spring.io/guides/gs/accessing-data-jpa/');
add('entities','Map your first entity',25,
"Let’s turn our Task class into something JPA can save. We’ll give it an ID, map its fields to columns, and add the constructor JPA needs.",
[['Map identity and columns','@Entity marks a persistent class. @Table chooses the table name. @Id marks its identifier, and @GeneratedValue asks the database/provider to generate it. Use Long for a generated ID so a new entity can represent “not assigned yet” with null.'],['Respect entity construction','JPA needs a no-argument constructor that is public or protected. Provide another constructor for normal application use. Keep fields private and expose meaningful behavior. Use jakarta.persistence imports for modern Boot, not older javax.persistence examples.'],['Know what annotations enforce','@Column(nullable=false) influences schema generation; it is not a replacement for request validation. The database constraint catches invalid stored data. @NotBlank on a request DTO provides earlier, more helpful feedback. Both can be useful at different boundaries.']],
code('Task.java — replaces the plain Java class from lesson 3','package com.example.taskapi;\n\nimport jakarta.persistence.*;\n\n@Entity\n@Table(name = "tasks")\npublic class Task {\n    @Id\n    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)\n    private Long id;\n\n    @Column(nullable = false, length = 120)\n    private String title;\n\n    @Column(nullable = false)\n    private boolean completed;\n\n    protected Task() {}\n    public Task(String title) { this.title = title; }\n\n    public Long getId() { return id; }\n    public String getTitle() { return title; }\n    public boolean isCompleted() { return completed; }\n\n    public void update(String title, boolean completed) {\n        this.title = title;\n        this.completed = completed;\n    }\n}'),
'New Task("Study") has no ID until persistence assigns one. completed defaults to false. The update method changes only editable fields. Avoid exposing an ID setter that allows callers to change identity.',
'Add a description field limited to 500 characters. Which other parts must change if the API should accept and return it?',
'Add a mapped field and access/update behavior, then change request DTOs, response DTOs, and mapping code. Add validation and tests. For a persistent production database, add a schema migration rather than relying on auto-update.',
'https://jakarta.ee/specifications/persistence/');
add('repositories','Repositories & CRUD',25,
"We need to save tasks and find them again. Spring Data can provide those basic operations from a repository interface, so we don’t have to write them all ourselves.",
[['Declare the contract','Extend JpaRepository<Task, Long>. The first generic type is the entity; the second is its ID type. You inherit save, findById, findAll, and delete. Spring discovers repository interfaces under the application package. You do not instantiate the interface with new.'],['Handle missing rows','findById returns Optional<Task>. Convert absence into a meaningful exception rather than dereferencing null. save can insert a new entity or merge an existing one depending on entity state; it is not simply an SQL INSERT alias. Prefer loading the existing entity before an update.'],['Derived queries','A method such as findByCompleted(boolean completed) lets Spring derive a query from the entity property name. A typo can cause startup failure. For complex queries use explicit JPQL, specifications, or a suitable custom repository. Bind query parameters rather than concatenating untrusted SQL.']],
code('TaskRepository.java','package com.example.taskapi;\n\nimport java.util.List;\nimport org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;\n\npublic interface TaskRepository extends JpaRepository<Task, Long> {\n    List<Task> findByCompleted(boolean completed);\n}\n\n// In a service method:\n// Task task = repository.findById(id)\n//     .orElseThrow(() -> new TaskNotFoundException(id));'),
'The method name refers to the Java field completed. TaskNotFoundException is a custom exception introduced in the error-handling lesson. JpaRepository already provides CRUD methods without requiring you to declare them again.',
'Write a repository signature that returns all tasks whose title contains text, ignoring case.',
'List<Task> findByTitleContainingIgnoreCase(String text);. Spring Data interprets the property Title and the ContainingIgnoreCase keywords. This query still needs testing against the actual database and consideration of indexes as data grows.',
'https://docs.spring.io/spring-data/jpa/reference/repositories/query-methods-details.html');
add('transactions','Transactions & safe updates',25,
"What happens if an operation changes two things in the database and fails halfway through? A transaction lets those changes succeed or roll back together.",
[['Put the boundary around the use case','Place @Transactional on service methods that perform related database operations. An update should load the task, check it, and change it within a transaction. With a managed entity, Hibernate detects changes and writes them when the transaction flushes; explicit save is not always required.'],['Know the default behavior','Spring transaction support commonly uses a proxy around your bean. Calling an annotated method from another method on the same instance can bypass that proxy. Unchecked exceptions normally trigger rollback; checked exceptions need deliberate rollback configuration. Avoid catching failures inside a transaction and silently returning success.'],['Think about concurrent changes','A transaction alone does not prevent every lost update. Two clients can read an old value and overwrite each other. A JPA @Version field enables optimistic locking; handle conflicts intentionally, often as 409 or with conditional HTTP requests. This capstone starts simple and does not implement a full concurrency protocol.']],
code('Inside TaskService — import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional','@Transactional\npublic TaskResponse update(Long id, UpdateTaskRequest request) {\n    Task task = repository.findById(id)\n        .orElseThrow(() -> new TaskNotFoundException(id));\n\n    task.update(request.title(), request.completed());\n    return new TaskResponse(\n        task.getId(), task.getTitle(), task.isCompleted());\n}'),
'The entity returned by the repository is managed within the transaction. Changing its fields is tracked. The controller must validate completed as non-null before this Boolean is unboxed; the complete project does so.',
'A service updates a task and writes an audit row. The audit write fails with an unchecked exception. What should happen if both writes share one transaction?',
'Both writes should roll back. Without a shared transaction, the task might be updated without its required audit record. Keep slow network calls outside database transactions where possible; a database rollback cannot undo an external email.',
'https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/data-access/transaction/declarative/annotations.html');
add('pagination','Pagination, relationships & migrations',30,
"Our small task list is easy to load all at once. Here’s what to change when there are more rows, related tables, or database changes you need to keep track of.",
[['Bound collection size','Returning every row eventually becomes slow and memory-intensive. Accept a page and size, cap the size, and specify stable sorting. A Page includes total counts, which can require an extra query. A Slice tells you whether another slice exists without necessarily counting the whole result. Return your own page DTO for a stable API contract.'],['Model relationships deliberately','A task may belong to a user through @ManyToOne. Avoid serializing a bidirectional entity graph directly: it can recurse, leak fields, or cause many queries. Lazy loading defers work until access; fetching a list and then accessing each relationship can cause an N+1 query problem. Fetch the needed data deliberately inside a transaction and map to DTOs.'],['Keep track of database changes','Use Flyway or Liquibase to version database migrations. A file such as V1__create_tasks.sql runs once and is recorded. Add a new migration for each change; do not edit one already applied in a shared environment. Use ddl-auto=validate in production after migrations, not create-drop or automatic update. Back up and test migration recovery.']],
code('Pagination fragment inside a service','int safeSize = Math.max(1, Math.min(size, 100));\nPageable request = PageRequest.of(\n    Math.max(page, 0), safeSize, Sort.by("id").ascending());\n\nPage<TaskResponse> result = repository.findAll(request)\n    .map(t -> new TaskResponse(t.getId(), t.getTitle(), t.isCompleted()));\n\n// Imports: org.springframework.data.domain.*\n// Map content, number, size, and totalElements to your page DTO.'));
lessons[19].explain='Pages are zero-indexed. Sorting by a unique ID prevents unstable ordering between equal values. This is an extension to the unpaginated capstone, not its existing response shape.';
lessons[19].exercise='Your database has 25 tasks and page size is 10. Which page indexes contain results? What happens if you load user details separately for each task?';
lessons[19].solution='Pages 0, 1, and 2 contain 10, 10, and 5 tasks. Page 3 is empty. Loading each user separately can cause one collection query plus many relationship queries; inspect SQL and use a targeted fetch strategy.';
lessons[19].reference='https://docs.spring.io/spring-data/jpa/reference/repositories/query-methods-details.html';
add('validation','Validate incoming data',22,
"Someone will eventually send a blank title or leave out a required field. Let’s catch that input before it reaches the code that saves a task.",
[['Declare constraints','Add the Validation dependency. @NotBlank rejects null, empty, and whitespace-only strings. @Size(max=120) limits length but permits null by itself. Combine constraints where needed. @NotNull on Boolean distinguishes a missing value from a legitimate false. Use jakarta.validation packages.'],['Trigger validation','Put @Valid on @RequestBody parameters. Without a trigger, annotations on a DTO do not automatically validate every object created in Java. Type conversion and malformed JSON are separate failures that can happen before constraint validation. Service callers outside HTTP may need their own validation strategy.'],['Separate input and business rules','“Title must not be blank” is an input rule. “Only the owner may edit the task” depends on authenticated identity and stored state. Validate both at the appropriate layer; a valid JSON body is not proof that the action is authorized.']],
code('CreateTaskRequest.java','package com.example.taskapi;\n\nimport jakarta.validation.constraints.NotBlank;\nimport jakarta.validation.constraints.Size;\n\npublic record CreateTaskRequest(\n    @NotBlank(message = "Title is required")\n    @Size(max = 120, message = "Title must be at most 120 characters")\n    String title\n) {}'),
'For UpdateTaskRequest apply the same title constraints and @NotNull to Boolean completed. In the controller use @Valid @RequestBody UpdateTaskRequest request. Validation failures should produce 400 with actionable field messages.',
'Send a title consisting only of spaces, a 121-character title, and a missing title. What should the API return?',
'All should return 400: NotBlank rejects spaces and missing/null values, while Size rejects a 121-character string. A 120-character nonblank title is allowed. Add tests at these boundaries instead of testing only a typical title.',
'https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/web/webmvc/mvc-controller/ann-validation.html');
add('errors','Helpful, consistent errors',25,
"When a request fails, the response should help the caller understand why. Let’s return useful errors for missing tasks and invalid input.",
[['Translate domain failures','Throw TaskNotFoundException when a task does not exist. Use @RestControllerAdvice with @ExceptionHandler methods to translate exceptions into HTTP responses centrally. Keep controllers free of repetitive try/catch blocks. A missing row is 404; a broken database connection is not.'],['Use problem details','Spring supports ProblemDetail for structured error responses with status, title, detail, and related fields. Add an errors property for field-validation messages. Keep the structure predictable. Never include passwords, SQL statements, or full exception traces in public details.'],['Log enough to investigate','Use structured application logging and correlation IDs in a deployed service. Log unexpected failures on the server while returning a safe error to the caller. Do not catch Exception and return 200 with an “error” string; that misleads clients and monitoring.']],
code('ApiErrors.java — missing-resource handler','package com.example.taskapi;\n\nimport org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;\nimport org.springframework.http.ProblemDetail;\nimport org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;\n\n@RestControllerAdvice\npublic class ApiErrors {\n    @ExceptionHandler(TaskNotFoundException.class)\n    public ProblemDetail missing(TaskNotFoundException ex) {\n        return ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(\n            HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND, ex.getMessage());\n    }\n}\n\n// Separate TaskNotFoundException.java in the same package:\n// public class TaskNotFoundException extends RuntimeException {\n//     public TaskNotFoundException(Long id) {\n//         super("Task " + id + " was not found");\n//     }\n// }'),
'The full project also handles MethodArgumentNotValidException and malformed JSON. The exception handler returns a status corresponding to its ProblemDetail. A controller advice does not replace security filter error handling.',
'An unknown ID and a syntactically invalid ID both fail. Why should they not necessarily share a status?',
'/api/tasks/999999 can be a valid request for a missing resource: 404. /api/tasks/abc cannot bind to the numeric ID contract: 400. Distinguishing them makes clients easier to debug.',
'https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/web/webmvc/mvc-ann-rest-exceptions.html');
add('testing','Unit & integration testing',30,
"Let’s check that the code does what we expect. We’ll start with a Java unit test, then test a request going through the whole application.",
[['Unit tests isolate behavior','JUnit runs tests and provides assertions. A plain unit test can construct a Task without starting Spring. Mockito can substitute a repository to test service logic, including missing IDs. Avoid a full application context when no framework behavior is involved.'],['Integration tests connect the pieces','@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment=RANDOM_PORT) starts the application on a free port. A real HTTP client can verify serialization, validation, status codes, and persistence together. Test slices such as @WebMvcTest and @DataJpaTest target smaller portions; Boot 4 modularized test support and some imports differ from Boot 3 tutorials. Use the reference for your chosen version.'],['Test failures, not only success','Verify create → read → update → delete, missing IDs, empty titles, and malformed JSON. Make tests independent: create their own records and avoid hard-coding generated IDs. H2 is useful, but a PostgreSQL integration suite with Testcontainers catches database-specific differences.']],
code('src/test/java/com/example/taskapi/TaskTest.java','package com.example.taskapi;\n\nimport org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;\nimport static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;\n\nclass TaskTest {\n    @Test\n    void aNewTaskIsIncompleteAndCanBeUpdated() {\n        Task task = new Task("Study");\n        assertFalse(task.isCompleted());\n\n        task.update("Study Spring", true);\n        assertEquals("Study Spring", task.getTitle());\n        assertTrue(task.isCompleted());\n    }\n}'),
'Run ./mvnw test (Windows: .\\mvnw.cmd test). This test loads no Spring context and makes no HTTP calls. The capstone adds a complete HTTP integration test. A green unit test alone does not prove your endpoint is mapped correctly.',
'Name a bug this unit test catches and a bug it cannot catch. Then run the capstone HTTP tests.',
'It catches a broken Task.update method. It cannot catch a wrong @GetMapping path, failed JSON conversion, or missing Spring bean. HTTP integration tests exercise those boundaries. The downloadable project provides them; run them locally.',
'https://docs.spring.io/spring-boot/reference/testing/index.html');
add('security','Authentication & authorization',30,
"Before other people use your API, you need to know who they are and which tasks they can access. Those are two separate checks: authentication and authorization.",
[['Understand the default change','Adding Spring Security changes request handling: endpoints become protected by default and a development credential may appear in logs. Do not publish with generated or hard-coded demo credentials. Use a configured identity provider or a deliberately designed user system. Hash stored passwords with a suitable password encoder; never store plaintext.'],['Select an authentication model','A browser application often uses a secure session cookie. An API can validate bearer access tokens from an OAuth2/OIDC provider using resource-server support. A JWT is a token format, not an authentication flow. Validate signature, issuer, expiry, and intended audience; decoding a token is not validation. Do not build your own token issuer for a beginner project.'],['Authorize the actual resource','A role check is not enough for ownership. When users have private tasks, associate tasks with a user and constrain reads/updates to the authenticated owner. Never trust an ownerId in the request body as proof of identity. Test unauthenticated, forbidden, and cross-user access. The downloadable base API deliberately has no authentication and is for local learning.']],
code('Session-oriented configuration fragment — add Spring Security dependency','@Bean\nSecurityFilterChain security(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {\n    http.authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth\n        .requestMatchers("/hello", "/error").permitAll()\n        .anyRequest().authenticated())\n        .formLogin(Customizer.withDefaults());\n    return http.build();\n}\n\n// Inside a @Configuration class. Imports:\n// org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean\n// org.springframework.security.config.Customizer\n// org.springframework.security.config.annotation.web.builders.HttpSecurity\n// org.springframework.security.web.SecurityFilterChain'),
'This shows a session login policy, not a complete user system. CSRF protection remains enabled, so write requests need a valid CSRF token. Adding this to the base project requires updating tests and provisioning users. It is not included in the downloadable local demo.',
'A user knows another user’s task ID. Is checking that the caller is logged in enough to allow an update?',
'No. Verify ownership or an explicit authorization rule before accessing that task. Querying by both task ID and authenticated owner ID helps constrain access. Choose a consistent 403 or 404 policy that avoids revealing private resource existence.',
'https://spring.io/guides/gs/securing-web/');
add('cors','CORS, CSRF & common traps',25,
"If a request works in Postman but fails in your browser, CORS might be involved. We’ll also cover CSRF, which solves a different problem and often gets confused with it.",
[['CORS controls browser reads','An origin consists of scheme, host, and port. A frontend on localhost:3000 calling localhost:8080 is cross-origin. Browsers enforce the server’s CORS policy and may send an OPTIONS preflight. curl does not enforce browser CORS, so curl success does not prove your frontend is configured correctly.'],['CSRF protects ambient credentials','With cookies, a browser may attach credentials automatically to a forged request. CSRF defenses require proof that a state-changing request originated from the intended application. Keep protection for cookie/session authentication. For a truly stateless API using bearer tokens only in Authorization headers, evaluate disabling CSRF deliberately; “REST” alone is not a reason.'],['Keep the policy narrow','Allow the exact frontend origins, needed methods, and headers. Wildcard origins do not combine with credentialed browser access. CORS is not authentication and does not block a malicious non-browser client. If Spring Security is present, integrate its CORS support so preflight is handled correctly.']],
code('Inside a WebMvcConfigurer configuration — development origin only','@Override\npublic void addCorsMappings(CorsRegistry registry) {\n    registry.addMapping("/api/**")\n        .allowedOrigins("http://localhost:3000")\n        .allowedMethods("GET", "POST", "PUT", "DELETE")\n        .allowedHeaders("Content-Type", "Authorization");\n}\n\n// Implement org.springframework.web.servlet.config.annotation.WebMvcConfigurer\n// Import org.springframework.web.servlet.config.annotation.CorsRegistry\n// With Spring Security also configure http.cors(Customizer.withDefaults()).'),
'This is a development configuration fragment, not a universal production policy. Change the origin to the deployed frontend when appropriate. Credentialed cookie requests also require explicit credentials configuration and CSRF handling.',
'Postman works but a browser request fails after an OPTIONS request. What should you inspect first?',
'Inspect the browser network panel, requested origin, preflight response, allowed methods/headers, and security-filter handling. Do not assume the controller is broken, and do not remove authentication to solve a cross-origin configuration issue.',
'https://docs.spring.io/spring-security/reference/servlet/integrations/cors.html');
add('production','Package & deploy responsibly',30,
"The app works on your laptop. To run it elsewhere, you’ll need a packaged application, a database that keeps its data, and settings for the host you choose.",
[['Build an executable application','Run Maven verify to compile, test, and package the application. Spring Boot repackages the JAR with its dependencies. Run it with java -jar. A host for this backend must support a Java process or container; a static website host cannot execute a Spring Boot JAR. This learning website does not run Java examples in your browser.'],['Replace local assumptions','Use PostgreSQL or another supported persistent database, add its JDBC driver, and configure connection settings through environment variables. Add Flyway and its database-specific support when required; create and test migrations before selecting ddl-auto=validate. Run with a production profile, not the H2 create-drop configuration.'],['Deploy a complete environment','Choose a host that supports Java 21 and your database, set secrets through its secret manager, configure the port, add HTTPS through the host/proxy, and verify health and authorization. Confirm restart behavior, database backups, and rollback procedures. Never expose the unauthenticated capstone as a private multi-user task service.']],
code('Build locally, then run the packaged JAR','# macOS / Linux; use .\\mvnw.cmd on Windows\n./mvnw verify\njava -jar target/task-api-0.0.1-SNAPSHOT.jar\n\n# application-prod.properties (after adding PostgreSQL + migrations)\nserver.port=${PORT:8080}\nspring.datasource.url=${DB_URL}\nspring.datasource.username=${DB_USER}\nspring.datasource.password=${DB_PASSWORD}\nspring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=validate\nspring.jpa.open-in-view=false'),
'The file name matches the capstone pom.xml. To select production, set SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=prod and supply DB_URL as a JDBC URL such as jdbc:postgresql://host:5432/tasks. These values alone do not provision a database or install its driver.',
'What must change before moving from the local H2 project to a persistent PostgreSQL deployment?',
'Add the PostgreSQL driver and migration dependencies; create migrations; provide a database and least-privilege credentials; use the production profile; configure authentication and ownership; test on PostgreSQL; set HTTPS, health checks, backups, and secrets. Do not keep create-drop.',
'https://docs.spring.io/spring-boot/reference/packaging/index.html');
add('observability','Logs, health & troubleshooting',25,
"When something breaks, start with the response and the logs. Let’s go through common errors and how to narrow down what’s causing them.",
[['Check what your application is doing','Spring Boot Actuator adds operational endpoints when you add its starter. Expose only the endpoints you need; health and metrics are operational data, not a reason to publish all internals. Secure management access and avoid exposing environment secrets. Logging records events; metrics show aggregate behavior; tracing follows work across services.'],['Work from symptoms','Connection refused: check whether the process is running and the port is right. 404: check mapping and resource existence. 400: check request shape and validation. 415: check Content-Type. 401/403: inspect authentication, authorization, and CSRF. 500: inspect server logs for the root cause. A startup failure is different from a request failure.'],['Use a short diagnostic loop','Reproduce with one request, inspect the status and logs, form one hypothesis, change one thing, and rerun the same request. If a bean is missing, check annotations, package scanning, and conditional configuration. If SQL fails, inspect schema, migration status, and the active database URL without logging passwords.']],
code('Configuration after adding spring-boot-starter-actuator','management.endpoints.web.exposure.include=health,info\nmanagement.endpoint.health.show-details=never\n\n# A health request (subject to your security policy)\n# GET /actuator/health\n# {"status":"UP"}'),
'Health UP means configured checks passed, not that every business operation is correct. Monitor latency, errors, and database connection availability as well. Liveness and readiness serve different purposes in orchestrated environments.',
'Your app starts, but all controller URLs return 404. Your controller is in com.other.api. What is your first hypothesis?',
'It is outside the default component scan. Move it under com.example.taskapi or explicitly configure a suitable scan boundary. Verify the actual path and HTTP method as well. Restart and rerun the same request.',
'https://docs.spring.io/spring-boot/reference/actuator/index.html');
add('interview','Explain it in an interview',25,
"Can you explain your code to someone else? Use these questions to practice. They’re study prompts, not a list of questions from a particular company’s interview.",
[['Spring versus Spring Boot','Spring provides the underlying programming framework, including dependency injection and web support. Boot provides conventions, starters, conditional auto-configuration, and executable application support around it. Boot does not replace Spring or write your business rules. Explain this distinction with your own controller example.'],['Describe one complete request','For POST /api/tasks: the HTTP request reaches the server and filter chain; MVC selects the controller; the body becomes a DTO and is validated; the controller calls the service; the service persists through the repository within a transaction; a response DTO becomes JSON with 201. Security filters can reject a request before the controller runs.'],['Explain why you chose an approach','Constructor injection makes dependencies explicit. DTOs separate the API from storage. Transactions protect a unit of database work. Unit tests are fast but do not prove wiring; integration tests are broader but cost more. A singleton bean must avoid unsafe per-request mutable state. Use one concrete failure example for each concept.']],
code('Practice aloud — 60 to 90 seconds each','1. What does @SpringBootApplication enable?\n2. Why use constructor injection?\n3. @Controller versus @RestController?\n4. @RequestParam versus @PathVariable?\n5. What happens when findById returns empty?\n6. When can @Transactional fail to apply?\n7. Authentication versus authorization?\n8. Why does a successful unit test not prove an API works?'),
'@Controller commonly participates in view rendering, while @RestController combines controller behavior with response-body semantics. A @Controller method can also return a body when explicitly annotated. Avoid saying one “can only return HTML”.',
'Explain why @Transactional on a method called from another method in the same object may not start the intended transaction.',
'In the usual proxy-based setup, transaction advice runs when the call crosses the Spring proxy. A direct self-call stays inside the object and bypasses that interception. Move the transactional operation behind an appropriate bean boundary or deliberately choose another supported approach.',
'https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/data-access/transaction/declarative/annotations.html');
add('next-steps','What to learn next',20,
"Once the Task API makes sense, pick one feature to add. These are useful next steps, along with the problems they help solve.",
[['Improve the app you already have','Extend the project with pagination, filtering, optimistic locking, PostgreSQL migrations, Testcontainers, and authenticated ownership. Learn how to read SQL and query plans. Add an API specification and keep it synchronized with behavior. Make each change small enough to test and explain.'],['Add capabilities when a problem calls for them','Caching can reduce repeated reads but requires an invalidation policy. Scheduling runs periodic work but needs coordination with multiple instances. Messaging decouples work but introduces retries, duplicate delivery, and idempotency. Async execution changes thread and transaction context. Learn these constraints before adding a queue or @Async.'],['When to look at WebFlux or microservices','Spring MVC usually uses a blocking request model. WebFlux uses a reactive model and requires nonblocking end-to-end thinking to get its benefits. Neither label guarantees speed. Microservices add network failures, distributed tracing, deployment coordination, and data consistency challenges. Start by measuring the single application.']],
code('Suggested extension order','1. Add bounded pagination and title filtering.\n2. Add PostgreSQL, Flyway, and Testcontainers.\n3. Add an identity provider and per-user task ownership.\n4. Test forbidden and cross-user access.\n5. Add optimistic locking for conflicting edits.\n6. Deploy with health checks, metrics, and backups.\n7. Measure a bottleneck before choosing caching or messaging.'),
'These features are things to add yourself; they aren’t included in the download. You don’t need to learn every Spring project before you can build something useful.',
'Choose one feature to add. Write down what it should do and one thing that could go wrong before you start coding.',
'For pagination: page=0&size=10 returns at most 10 tasks in stable ID order; size=10000 is capped or rejected; pages beyond the end are empty; total counts are correct. Test ties and invalid page inputs rather than checking only one happy path.',
'https://spring.io/guides');
add('capstone','Capstone: your complete Task API',60,
"Here’s the complete Task API project. Download it, run it locally, and compare it with your own code. You’ll also find tests and instructions for trying every endpoint.",
[['What is included','The project contains a Maven build for Java 21 and Boot 4.1.1, application configuration, an H2-backed Task entity, repository, transactional service, REST controller, validated DTOs, problem-detail errors, a unit test, and HTTP integration tests. There is no account system, pagination, or production database. The API runs locally; the guide is not a Java execution environment.'],['Run the project','Download and unzip the project below. Install Java 21 and Maven 3.6.3 or later. The download uses system Maven and does not include a Maven wrapper. In the folder containing pom.xml, run <code>mvn verify</code>, then <code>mvn spring-boot:run</code>. Alternatively, copy its source and pom.xml into your Initializr project while keeping the generated wrapper, and use ./mvnw or .\\mvnw.cmd. Dependency downloads require internet access.'],['Prove the whole flow','Create a task with POST /api/tasks. Copy the generated ID. Read it with GET, replace its editable fields with PUT, delete it, then verify GET returns 404. Send a blank title and verify 400. Restart and confirm H2 data disappears. The code is provided for you to run and test locally; Java execution was not performed when publishing this guide.'],['Completion checklist','You can explain the request flow without reading notes; distinguish Java, Spring, and Boot; write and validate a controller; model persistence; handle missing records; run tests; explain transaction boundaries; and identify what must change before production. If one item feels shaky, revisit its lesson and repeat the exercise.']],
code('macOS / Linux end-to-end requests (replace 1 with the returned ID)','curl -i -X POST http://localhost:8080/api/tasks \\\n  -H \'Content-Type: application/json\' \\\n  -d \'{"title":"Finish the field guide"}\'\n\ncurl -i http://localhost:8080/api/tasks/1\n\ncurl -i -X PUT http://localhost:8080/api/tasks/1 \\\n  -H \'Content-Type: application/json\' \\\n  -d \'{"title":"Finish the field guide","completed":true}\'\n\ncurl -i -X DELETE http://localhost:8080/api/tasks/1\ncurl -i http://localhost:8080/api/tasks/1'),
'Expected statuses in order: 201, 200, 200, 204, 404. The download includes Windows PowerShell equivalents in README.md and integration tests that derive the ID from the creation Location header.',
'Build a similar Book API without copying the controller. A book has a generated ID, a nonblank title, and an author. Write the endpoint contract first.',
'Create Book entity, create/update/response DTOs, repository, service, and controller. Validate title and author, return 201 plus Location for creation, use 404 for missing IDs, and test the full lifecycle plus invalid input. Reuse the architecture, then explain every dependency.',
'https://spring.io/guides');
